<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Tyrol</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alto Adige</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> is the German-speaking Alpine region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> attached it to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1810, and it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1815. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> annexed it in 1919, despite the claim of German-Austria, and it is now an autonomous province of Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-tyrol.jpg" alt="South Tyrol" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Tyrol_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
